                  %div
                    %p
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/edit/foundations+-+contents"} Edit this sidebar
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definition"} Definition
              %li
                %a{:href => "#a_constructive_view"} A constructive view
              %li
                %a{:href => "#a_contemporary_perspective"} A contemporary perspective
              %li
                %a{:href => "#ExpositionByTrimble"} Todd Trimble’s exposition of ETCS
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_entries"} Related entries
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            = succeed "," do
              %strong Elementary Theory of the Category of Sets
            %em ETCS
            for short, is an axiomatic formulation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category-theoretic
            spirit. As such, it is the prototypical
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+set+theory"} structural set theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCC"} ETCC
            in (
            %a{:href => "#Lawvere64"}> Lawvere 64
            ) it is also the paradigm for a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} categorical foundation
            of mathematics.
            %sup#fnref:1
              %a{:href => "#fn:1", :rel => "footnote"} 1
          %p
            The theory intends to capture in an invariant way the notion of a (constant)
            %em ‘abstract set’
            whose elements lack internal structure and whose only external property is cardinality with further external relations arising from
            = succeed "." do
              %em mappings
            %em local
            and
            %em relative
            i.e. membership is meaningful only between an element of a set and a subset of the very same set. (See
            %a{:href => "#Lawvere76"} Lawvere (1976, p.119)
            for a detailed description of the notion ‘abstract set’.
            %sup#fnref:2
              %a{:href => "#fn:2", :rel => "footnote"} 2
            %sup#fnref:3
              %a{:href => "#fn:3", :rel => "footnote"} 3
            %sup#fnref:4
              %a{:href => "#fn:4", :rel => "footnote"} 4
            = succeed ")" do
              %sup#fnref:5
                %a{:href => "#fn:5", :rel => "footnote"} 5
          %p
            More in detail, ETCS is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+theory"} first-order theory
            axiomatizing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+toposes"} elementary toposes
            and specifically those which are
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-pointed+topos"} well-pointed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+object"} natural numbers object
            and satisfy the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+replacement"} axiom of replacement
          %p
            The idea is, first of all, that much of traditional mathematics naturally takes place “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"}> inside
            ” such a topos of
            %em constant
            sets, and second that this perspective generalizes beyond ETCS proper to toposes of
            %em variable
            and
            %em cohesive
            sets by varying the axioms: for instance omitting the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-pointed+topos"} well-pointedness
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            but adding the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kock-Lawvere+axiom"} Kock-Lawvere axiom
            gives a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+topos"} smooth topos
            inside which
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
            takes place.
          %p
            That is, ETCS locates the category of sets by the well-pointedness axiom as the discrete zero point on a ‘continuous’ range of toposes eligible for foundations. In particular, whereas
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            mainly provides ‘substance’ for mathematics, ETCS lives as a special type of form within the continuum of mathematical form itself.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p The axioms of ETCS can be summed up in one sentence as:
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} category of sets
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+the"} the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
              which
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  is a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-pointed+topos"} well-pointed topos
              %li
                %p
                  has a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+object"} natural numbers object
              %li
                %p
                  and satisfies the
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
          %p For more details see
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+formal+ETCS"}> fully formal ETCS
              \.
          %h2#a_constructive_view A constructive view
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Erik+Palmgren"} Erik Palmgren
            (
            %a{:href => "#Palmgren"}> Palmgren 2012
            ) has a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicative+mathematics"} predicative
            variant of ETCS, which can be summarized as:
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-pointed+topos"} well-pointed
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%CE%A0-pretopos"} pretopos
            with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/NNO"} NNO
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enough+projectives"} enough projectives
            (i.e.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/COSHEP"} COSHEP
            is satisfied). Here “well-pointed” must be taken in its constructive sense, as including that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
            is indecomposable and projective.
          %h2#a_contemporary_perspective A contemporary perspective
          %p
            Modern mathematics with its emphasis on concepts from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            would more directly be founded in a similar spirit by an axiomatization not just of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+toposes"} elementary toposes
            but of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} elementary (∞,1)-toposes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence"} univalent
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            accomplishes – for more on this see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "relation+between+type+theory+and+category+theory#HomotopyWithUnivalence"} relation between type theory and category theory – Univalent HoTT and Elementary infinity-toposes
          %p
            Instead of increasing the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher categorical dimension
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28n%2Cr%29-category"} (n,r)
            in the first argument, one may also, in this context of elementary foundations, consider raising the second argument. The case
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (2,2)
            is the elementary theory of the 2-category of categories (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCC"}> ETCC
            ).
          %h2#ExpositionByTrimble Todd Trimble’s exposition of ETCS
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Todd+Trimble"} Todd Trimble
            has a series of expository writings on ETCS which provide a very careful introduction and at the same time a wealth of useful details.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Todd Trimble,
                %em ZFC and ETCS: Elementary Theory of the Category of Sets
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Trimble+on+ETCS+I"}> nLab entry
                ,
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://topologicalmusings.wordpress.com/2008/09/01/zfc-and-etcs-elementary-theory-of-the-category-of-sets/"} original blog entry
            %li
              %p
                Todd Trimble,
                %em ETCS: Internalizing the logic
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Trimble+on+ETCS+II"}> nLab entry
                ,
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://topologicalmusings.wordpress.com/2008/09/10/etcs-internalizing-the-logic/"} original blog entry
            %li
              %p
                Todd Trimble,
                %em ETCS: Building joins and coproducts
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Trimble+on+ETCS+III"}> nLab entry
                ,
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://topologicalmusings.wordpress.com/2008/12/15/etcs-building-joins-and-coproducts/"} original blog entry
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+formal+ETCS"} fully formal ETCS
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS+with+elements"} ETCS with elements
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+set+theory"} structural set theory
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} Zermelo Fraenkel set theory
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cohesive+Toposes+and+Cantor%27s+%22lauter+Einsen%22"} Cohesive Toposes and Cantor's "lauter Einsen"
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+replacement"} axiom of replacement
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCC"} ETCC
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/practical+foundations+of+mathematics"} practical foundations of mathematics
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations+of+mathematics"} foundations of mathematics
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCR"} ETCR
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mostowski+set+theory"} Mostowski set theory
          %h2#references References
          %p ETCS grew out of Lawvere’s experiences of teaching undergraduate foundations of analysis at Reed college in 1963 and was originally published in
          %ul
            %li#Lawvere64
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
              ,
              %em An elementary theory of the category of sets
              , Proceedings of the National Academy of Science of the U.S.A
              %strong 52
              pp.1506-1511 (1964). (
              %a{:href => "http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC300477/pdf/pnas00186-0196.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p A more or less contemporary review is
          %ul
            %li
              C.C. Elgot,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Review
              %strong 37
              no.1 (1972) pp. 191-192.
          %p A longer version of Lawvere’s 1964 paper appears in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Colin+McLarty"} Colin McLarty
              %em An elementary theory of the category of sets (long version) with commentary
              , Reprints in Theory and Applications of Categories
              %strong 11
              (2005) pp. 1-35. (
              %a{:href => "http://tac.mta.ca/tac/reprints/articles/11/tr11abs.html"}> TAC
              )
          %p An undergraduate set-theory textbook using it is
          %ul
            %li#LawvereRosebrugh
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Rosebrugh"} Robert Rosebrugh
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sets+for+Mathematics"} Sets for Mathematics
              , CUP 2003. (
              %a{:href => "http://books.google.de/books?id=h3_7aZz9ZMoC&pg=PP1&dq=sets+for+mathematics"}> web
              )
          %p Lawvere explains in detail his views on constant and variable ‘abstract sets’ on pp.118-128 of
          %ul
            %li#Lawvere76
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
              ,
              %em Variable Quantities and Variable Structures in Topoi
              , pp.101-131 in Heller, Tierney (eds.),
              %em Algebra, Topology and Category Theory: a Collection of Papers in Honor of Samuel Eilenberg
              , Academic Press New York 1976.
          %p See also ch. 2,3 of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
              ,
              %em Variable Sets Entendu and Variable Structure in Topoi
              , lecture notes University of Chicago 1975.
          %p
            On the anticipation of ‘abstract sets’ in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Cantor"} Cantor
          %ul
            %li#Lawvere94
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cohesive+Toposes+and+Cantor%27s+%22lauter+Einsen%22"} Cohesive Toposes and Cantor's "lauter Einsen"
              %strong 2
              no.3 (1994) pp.5-15. (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/LawvereCohesiveToposes.pdf", :title => "pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p A short overview article on ETCS:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tom+Leinster"}> Tom Leinster
              ,
              %em Rethinking set theory
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1212.6543"} arXiv
          %p An insightful and non-partisan view of ETCS can be found in a section of:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andreas+Blass"}> Andreas Blass
              , Yuri Gurevich,
              %em Why Sets ?
              , Bull. Europ. Assoc. Theoret. Comp. Sci.
              %strong 84
              (2004) 139-156. (
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.lsa.umich.edu/~ablass/set.pdf"}> draft
              )
          %p An extended discussion from a philosophical perspective is in
          %ul
            %li#McLarty04
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Colin+McLarty"}> Colin McLarty
                ,
                %em Exploring Categorical Structuralism
                , Phil. Math.
                %strong 12
                (2004) 37-53.
            %li
              %p
                Øystein Linnebo, Richard Pettigrew,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Category theory as an autonomous foundation
                %strong 19
                (2011) 227–254 (
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1093/philmat/nkr024"}> doi:10.1093/philmat/nkr024
                ,
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://philsci-archive.pitt.edu/5392/1/onlyuptoiso.pdf"} preprint version (pdf)
          %p For a more recent review from a critical perspective containing additional recent references see
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Solomon+Feferman"}> Solomon Feferman
              ,
              %em Foundations of Unlimited Category Theory: What Remains to be Done
              , Review of Symbolic Logic
              %strong 6
              no.1 (2013) 6-15. (
              %a{:href => "http://math.stanford.edu/~feferman/papers/FCT-RSL-2013.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p An informative discussion of the pros and cons of Lawvere’s approach can be found in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Pierre+Marquis"}> Jean-Pierre Marquis
              ,
              %em Kreisel and Lawvere on Category Theory and the Foundations of Mathematics
              \. (
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.mcgill.ca/rags/seminar/Marquis_KreiselLawvere.pdf"}> pdf-slides
              )
          %p Palmgren’s ideas can be found here:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Erik+Palmgren"}> Erik Palmgren
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Constructivist and Structuralist Foundations: Bishop’s and Lawvere’s Theories of Sets
              %strong 163
              10 (2012) 1384-1399 (2009) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.apal.2012.01.011"}> doi:10.1016/j.apal.2012.01.011
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1201.6272"} arXiv:1201.6272
          %p
            For the relation between the theory of well-pointed toposes and
            %strong weak Zermelo set theory
            as elucidated by work of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Julian+Cole"} J. Cole
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Barry+Mitchell"} Barry Mitchell
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gerhard+Osius"} G. Osius
            in the early 1970s see
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
                ,
                %em Topos Theory
                , Academic Press New York 1977 (Dover reprint 2014). (sections 9.2-3)
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Barry+Mitchell"}> Barry Mitchell
                ,
                %em Boolean Topoi and the Theory of Sets
                , JPAA
                %strong 2
                (1972) pp.261-274.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gerhard+Osius"}> Gerhard Osius
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Categorical Set Theory: A Characterization of the Category of Sets
                %strong 4
                (1974) 79-119.
          .footnotes
            %hr/
            %ol
              %li#fn:1
                %p
                  For a comparative discussion of its virtues as foundation see
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations+of+mathematics"} foundations of mathematics
                  , the
                  %a{:href => "#ExpositionByTrimble"} texts by Todd Trimble
                  or the informative paper by
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a{:href => "#McLarty04"} McLarty (2004)
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:1", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
              %li#fn:2
                %p
                  It has been pointed out by John Myhill that Cantor’s concept of ‘cardinal’ as a set of abstract units should be viewed as a structural set theory and a precursor to Lawvere’s concept of an ‘abstract set’. This view is endorsed and expanded in
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a{:href => "#Lawvere94"} Lawvere 1994
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:2", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
              %li#fn:3
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Dedekind"} R. Dedekind's
                  views are also anticipating ‘abstract sets’ e.g. Bernstein reports in Dedekind’s works vol.3 (1932, p.449) that Dedekind gave as his intuition of a set: “a closed bag, containing determinate things that one can not see and of which one knows nothing beyond their existence and determinateness”.
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:3", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
              %li#fn:4
                %p
                  The first axiomatic set theory without primitive membership relation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∈
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in
                  was presumably proposed by A. Schoenflies in 1920: he modeled elements of sets as indecomposable subsets. See A. Schoenflies,
                  %em Zur Axiomatik der Mengenlehre
                  , Math. Ann.
                  %strong 83
                  (1921) pp.173-200; and
                  %em Bemerkung zur Axiomatik der Grössen und Mengen
                  , Math. Ann.
                  %strong 85
                  (1922) pp.60-64.
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:4", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
              %li#fn:5
                %p
                  The first axiomatic set theory based on the notion of function was
                  = succeed "\u2018s" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+von+Neumann"} von Neumann
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/NBG"} NBG
                  theory of classes.
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:5", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
        .revisedby
